(Stage 1e1 review — nesting combined with the other tags. A nested list
 holding a float exercises PRINT_FLOAT inside the recursive frame; an
 empty child must render as [] rather than being skipped.
 Was also a MUTUAL-cycle test (aa/bb appending each other, hitting the
 depth bound and flagging an error). docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #111 (owner
 ruling, GitHub #34, Option 1): a collection placed inside another
 collection is now a COPY, so `append aa to bb` then `append bb to aa`
 copies each list's state at the moment of ITS OWN append rather than
 sharing a pointer - no cycle can form. `aa` ends up three levels deep
 (a copy of bb, which holds a copy of aa's original empty state), not
 infinite, and no error is set.)

a list called inner is [1.5, "s", yes].
a list called outer is [].
append 7 to outer.
append inner to outer.
append 2.5 to outer.
print outer.
If element 2 of outer is a list, print "elem2 is list".
Otherwise print "elem2 not list".

a list called empty_child is [].
a list called holder is [].
append empty_child to holder.
print holder.

a list called aa is [].
a list called bb is [].
append aa to bb.
append bb to aa.
print aa.
on error print "cycle-flagged".
